# reachability.baseline — functions no production entry point can reach.
#
# Each line is: <GOOS>/<GOARCH> <package-import-path>.<function>
# Rooted at the cmd/** main packages only. Test binaries are NOT roots, so a
# name here may still be reached by a test — that is the point.
#
# The two configurations agree name-for-name today, so every entry appears
# twice. That is the evidence, not waste: no production file is currently
# platform-scoped. The day one is, the two lists diverge and say so.
#
# A name in this file is one of three things, and the difference matters:
#   1. A test seam (a fake clock, a recording emitter). Correct. Leave it.
#   2. Inert leftovers — a superseded wrapper, an unused accessor. Harmless.
#   3. A protection that cannot protect — a guard, gate, lock, reaper or
#      detector that production never calls. That one is a defect.
#
# Adding a line means accepting one of those three. Removing lines is always
# welcome: it means something got wired up or deleted.
#
# This file has no room for a reason, and --write-baseline rewrites it whole.
# So say WHY in the commit body of the commit that adds a line. Without that,
# a real defect ratified in the same week as a real test seam is indistinguishable
# from it forever after.
#
# Re-ratify deliberately with: scripts/reachability-gate.sh --write-baseline
